-- ***** SOUNDNESS WITNESS -- read before touching the Float/Z3 encoding *****
--
-- Refinement types, Float encoding (§2.14; see specs/progress.md "Float
-- refinements via Z3 bit-precise FloatingPoint" and the float_suite comment
-- in test/test_refinecheck.ml: "Discharged through Z3's BIT-PRECISE
-- FloatingPoint theory ... never through Real. Over reals trichotomy makes
-- `not(x >= 0) && not(x <= 0)` unsatisfiable ... over floats it is
-- satisfiable, witnessed by NaN.").
--
-- `weird`'s precondition is `not (_ >= 0.0) && not (_ <= 0.0)`. Over the REAL
-- numbers this is a contradiction: trichotomy guarantees every real is `>= 0`
-- or `<= 0` (or both, at exactly 0), so no real value can fail both
-- disjuncts. If refinecheck encoded `Float` as Z3's `Real` sort, this
-- predicate would be UNSATISFIABLE, and an unconstrained argument passed to
-- `weird` would let Z3 prove `not (feasible)` -- i.e. report a refinement
-- violation on code that never supplies a violating value, because no value
-- of the DECLARED type can even reach `weird` without already being
-- impossible. That is a false positive baked into the encoding itself, not a
-- bug in any particular call.
--
-- Under IEEE-754 semantics (Z3's bit-precise `FloatingPoint`/`Float64` sort,
-- `fp.geq`/`fp.leq`), the predicate IS satisfiable: `NaN` compares `false`
-- against every ordered relation, including `>=` and `<=`, so `NaN` satisfies
-- BOTH `not (_ >= 0.0)` and `not (_ <= 0.0)` simultaneously. Because the
-- argument here is an unconstrained parameter `k` (not a literal), Z3 cannot
-- rule out `k = NaN` reaching `weird`, so refinecheck correctly stays SILENT
-- -- this file must `--check` exit 0.
--
-- WHAT BREAKS IF THIS EVER FLIPS: if a future change "simplifies" the Float
-- encoding to Z3's `Real` sort (dropping NaN/infinity semantics because they
-- seem like an edge case), this exact file -- calling `weird` with an
-- ordinary unconstrained `Float` parameter -- would start being reported as
-- a refinement violation, even though the call is correct and the compiler
-- accepted the same shape as accept/t99 moments earlier. This witness exists
-- to fail the moment that simplification lands.
mod M do
  fn weird(x : {Float | not (_ >= 0.0) && not (_ <= 0.0)}) : Float do x end
  fn v(k : Float) : Float do weird(k) end
end
